text:
maria: Heads up — noticed SDK sample app builds hanging on integration tests on API 30 emulator. Root cause looks like a leaked OkHttp dispatcher/executor after coroutine cancellation.

li: Interesting. What do the symptoms look like? Hanging threads or out-of-memory?

maria: Hanging tests. The Gradle worker shows many stuck threads in `OkHttp Dispatcher` executor, and subsequent network calls block forever. `adb shell dumpsys` shows sockets in CLOSE_WAIT.

dave: Is this the adapter that runs `Call.enqueue()` then resumes a suspended coroutine on callback? We've seen subtle leaks when the app cancels the coroutine but the callback still holds a reference.

maria: We're using a small custom adapter that wraps `call.enqueue()` and uses `suspendCancellableCoroutine` to resume onResponse/onFailure. The pattern seems right, but when the coroutine is cancelled we call `call.cancel()`. Still, threads accumulate under heavy concurrency in tests.

li: Can you paste the adapter code?

maria: Sure:

```
class SuspendCallAdapter : CallAdapter<Any, Any> {
  override fun adapt(call: Call<Any>): Any {
    return suspendCancellableCoroutine { cont ->
      call.enqueue(object : Callback {
        override fun onFailure(call: Call<Any>, e: IOException) { cont.resumeWithException(e) }
        override fun onResponse(call: Call<Any>, resp: Response<Any>) { cont.resume(resp) }
      })
      cont.invokeOnCancellation { call.cancel() }
    }
  }
}
```

li: That looks standard. Are you creating new OkHttpClient instances per test? That could starve the system with many executor threads. OkHttp's Dispatcher creates an ExecutorService per client if none is provided.

maria: We do create a fresh client per test to isolate config. But tests don't shut down the client. Should calling `call.cancel()` free the executor? I thought OkHttp cleaned up idle thread pools eventually.

dave: It will if the ConnectionPool and Dispatcher are idle and GC runs, but in unit/integration test harnesses GC isn't reliable. Best practice: supply a shared Dispatcher/Executor or call `client.dispatcher.executorService.shutdown()` and `client.connectionPool.evictAll()` in teardown.

maria: Good point. Also saw weird interaction with `callTimeout` — when we set `callTimeout(60, TimeUnit.SECONDS)` some requests that were cancelled still caused `onFailure` with `SocketTimeoutException` later and leaked the callback. If cancellation races with timeout, callback threads keep running and hold references.

li: Right. There is a known ordering: cancellation may interrupt the write/read but the dispatcher may already queued the callback. You should protect the callback resume with an `AtomicBoolean` to ensure only one path resumes the continuation.

maria: Implemented this guard and added explicit client teardown in tests. Fixed the hang. Example guard I used:

```
val resumed = AtomicBoolean(false)
call.enqueue(object: Callback {
  override fun onFailure(call: Call, e: IOException) {
    if (resumed.compareAndSet(false, true)) cont.resumeWithException(e)
  }
  override fun onResponse(call: Call, r: Response) {
    if (resumed.compareAndSet(false, true)) cont.resume(r)
  }
})
cont.invokeOnCancellation {
  if (resumed.compareAndSet(false, true)) call.cancel()
}
```

maria: After adding that plus a shared `Dispatcher(Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8))` passed into the OkHttpClient builder in tests, flakiness disappeared.

dave: Nice. We should add a short note in the devex docs about test harnesses: 1) prefer shared dispatcher in tests, 2) always teardown clients (shutdown executor, evict connection pool), 3) guard continuation resumption to avoid double-resume when cancellation races with callbacks.

li: Also mention `callTimeout` vs `readTimeout` semantics — `callTimeout` applies to the entire call life and will enqueue a cancel which can race with normal responses. If teams rely on strict ordering, they need the AtomicBoolean pattern.

